! Overview

File Pipeline provides configurable binary data processing pipelines for TiddlyWiki. It supports two types of processing steps:

* ''Command steps'' — run shell commands like ImageMagick, pdftotext, pandoc, or LibreOffice
* ''LLM steps'' — send extracted text to an LLM for analysis (auto-background or interactive with human-in-the-loop)

!! Architecture

The plugin has three layers:

* ''`pipeline-executor.js`'' (server) — loads pipeline definitions, runs command steps via `runner-actions.json`
* ''`pipeline-route.js`'' (server) — `POST /api/file-pipeline` endpoint, callable from any plugin
* ''`pipeline-client.js`'' (browser) — orchestrates the full flow: server call → artifact creation → LLM steps

!! Integration

When installed alongside ''file-upload'', all `<$file-dropzone>` widgets automatically trigger matching pipelines on upload. The file-upload thumbnail settings are replaced by file-pipeline's settings.

When installed alongside ''llm-connect'', document extraction uses pipelines instead of the legacy `/api/extract` route. Cached `.extracted` tiddlers are shared between both systems.

!! Pipeline Schema

Pipelines are defined as JSON tiddlers tagged `$:/tags/rimir/file-pipeline/pipeline`:

```json
{
  "name": "pipeline-name",
  "match": ["mime/type"],
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "step-id",
      "action": "runner-action-name",
      "input": "source",
      "output": "_generated/{{name}}_thumb.png",
      "captureStdout": false,
      "scanDir": false,
      "condition": {"config": "$:/config/...", "equals": "yes"},
      "params": {"resolution": "{{config:$:/config/...}}"},
      "artifact": {"setField": "_thumbnail_uri"}
    }
  ]
}
```

!! Step Chaining

Steps reference each other via `"input": "step:<id>"`. The output of a command step (file path or captured stdout) becomes the input of the next step. This enables multi-stage workflows like DOC → DOCX → PDF → thumbnail.

!! LLM Steps

LLM steps send text from a previous step to an LLM API and store the response as an artifact. Two modes:

* ''Auto'' (`"mode": "auto"`) — runs in the background via `orchestrator.runAction()`, no user interaction
* ''Interactive'' (`"mode": "interactive"`) — opens a chat panel, user can change model, edit prompt, iterate, then save

LLM step fields: `promptTemplate` (tiddler title with `{{text}}`/`{{title}}`/`{{filename}}` substitution), `systemPrompt`, `provider`, `model`, `maxTokens`.

!! LLM Configuration Priority

LLM settings are resolved in this order (highest priority first):

# ''Step level'' — `systemPrompt`, `provider`, `model` fields in the pipeline step JSON (supports `{{config:...}}` indirection)
# ''Dropzone level'' — `prop-system-prompt`, `prop-provider`, `prop-model` attributes on `<$file-dropzone>`
# ''Global default'' — llm-connect's provider/model/system-prompt settings

This allows a single pipeline definition to be reused across different dropzones with different LLM configurations.

!! Artifact Lifecycle

All generated tiddlers carry `_artifact_source` (pointing to the original file) and `_artifact_type` fields. The file-upload plugin's cascade system automatically handles delete and rename propagation.

!! Template variables in command actions

The runner-action `command` template (in `runner-actions.json` or the bundled `$:/config/rimir/file-pipeline/default-actions`) can reference three built-in variables, all auto-quoted with double quotes by the executor so paths with spaces survive intact:

|!Variable |!Resolves to |
|`{{input}}` |Absolute filesystem path of the source file (or, for chained steps, the prior step's output path) |
|`{{output}}` |Absolute filesystem path of the step's resolved `output` template (after `path.resolve`, which strips trailing slashes) |
|`{{canonical_uri}}` |The source tiddler's `_canonical_uri` (the served URL prefix; e.g. `/files/email/foo.msg` for the default location, `/work/.../foo.msg` for orga-apps). Use this to derive served URLs for any files the action writes — the filesystem path is not the URL for non-default file-upload locations |

User-defined step `params` are substituted into the command as `{{paramname}}` AFTER the built-ins; they are NOT auto-quoted, so quote them explicitly in the action template if they might contain spaces or shell metacharacters.

!! Artifact deserialization

`createTextArtifact` (`pipeline-client.js`) routes captured stdout through `$tw.wiki.deserializeTiddlers(type, text, {})` before storing the artifact. Any `tiddlerdeserializer` registered for the artifact's `type` runs, so embedded metadata becomes real tiddler fields. For `text/x-frontmattered-markdown` artifacts (e.g. `rimir/msg-import`'s `.email` output) this means YAML frontmatter at the top of the body is parsed into fields instead of staying as literal text. Types without a registered deserializer fall back to the default (text-only) — backward-compatible with existing pipelines that produce plain `text/x-markdown`.

!! Per-output MIME inference for multi-file artifacts

Multi-file scanDir steps (`createMultiFileArtifacts` in `pipeline-client.js`) used to default every output's tiddler `type` to `image/png`, regardless of the file extension. Since 0.1.10, each output's MIME is inferred from its filename using a built-in extension table plus `$tw.config.fileExtensionInfo` as a fallback. PDF, .msg, .docx etc. attachments now arrive as their real types — downstream MIME-branching UIs (e.g. orga-apps' attachment grid) finally render them correctly. Pipeline definitions can still pin a fixed `tiddlerType` on an `artifact` block to override the inference.

!! Nested-pipeline triggering and cycle protection

After each artifact is created, `createMultiFileArtifacts` invokes the browser-side hook `rimir-file-pipeline-artifact-created` with `{title, type, uri, sourceTitle, seen}`. The bundled `nested-trigger-startup` module listens on the hook: if the artifact's MIME matches a registered pipeline AND its URI is not already in the recursion's `seen` map, the module calls `pipeline-client.runPipeline` for it with an extended `seen`. The result: a PDF attachment of a `.msg` gets the PDF pipeline (thumbnail + text extraction) automatically; an attached `.msg` gets its own `.email` recursively. Cycle protection uses `seen` keyed by URI — a `.msg` that references itself by content short-circuits instead of looping forever.

Recursion depth is bounded in practice by title prefixes (each level adds `.attachments/<name>` to the parent's title) and by file-upload's content-hash dedup. There is no hard depth cap.

!! App-context field inheritance

Since 0.1.11, artifact creation also copies the source tiddler's `pa.parent` field onto the new artifact (when present). This is the orga-apps "note anchor" convention: a tiddler uploaded into a note carries `pa.parent: <note-title>` so the note's attachment grid surfaces it. Without inheritance, pipeline-produced artifacts (PDF attachments of a `.msg` dropped onto a note, attached `.msg` body extractions, etc.) would be tracked correctly via `_artifact_source` but invisible to the grid. The mechanism is implemented in `inheritContextFields(sourceTitle)` — extending the rule to additional namespace fields is a one-line change.